# Which modules the initramfs carries — the kernel-modules-irf payload.
#
# One path per line, relative to lib/modules/<release>/kernel/. A directory
# takes everything beneath it. Blank lines and # comments are ignored. A listed
# path that does not exist is skipped, so this survives config changes that
# retire a subsystem.
#
# GENERIC, NOT HOSTONLY. This set is the same on every machine. Peios cannot
# build a hostonly initramfs even if it wanted to: peipkg-compose resolves
# offline and runs no install-time side effects, so nothing in the compose path
# may probe the hardware it is composing for. A per-machine initramfs would also
# have to be assembled and signed on the target, since the UKI is signed whole —
# which would put a signing key on every installed machine. Generic is the
# mainstream default anyway (Debian's MODULES=most, Fedora's non-hostonly
# dracut); hostonly is the opt-in, not the norm.
#
# THE RULE FOR THIS FILE: the initramfs only has to reach the root filesystem.
# Anything the real root can load for itself once it is mounted does not belong
# here — GPU, sound, media, wifi, sensors, infiniband. Adding a subsystem here
# costs image size on every machine forever, so the bar is "the boot cannot
# proceed without it", not "a machine might have one".

# --- Reaching the root device -------------------------------------------
# Storage controllers, block devices and the transports they sit behind.
drivers/ata
drivers/block
drivers/nvme
drivers/scsi
drivers/md
drivers/message
drivers/mmc
drivers/firewire
drivers/mtd
drivers/nvmem
drivers/virtio
drivers/usb
drivers/pci
drivers/base
drivers/char

# --- Unlocking it -------------------------------------------------------
# Keyboards, so a human can type a passphrase for an encrypted root. USB
# keyboards also need the host controllers above.
drivers/hid
drivers/input

# --- Mounting it --------------------------------------------------------
# Filesystem drivers, the crypto the encrypted case needs, and the shared
# helpers (crc, compression) both pull in.
fs
crypto
lib
arch

# --- Network ------------------------------------------------------------
# For a root filesystem reached over the network. Peios has no netboot or NFS
# root today — live-boot mounts the boot medium, disk-boot mounts a disk — so
# these two lines currently serve a root type that does not exist, and they are
# 49 MB and 1182 modules of the set, more than the rest combined. Deleting them
# is the single biggest size lever here. They are kept because MODULES=most is
# defined to include them and dropping them is a decision about which root
# types Peios intends to support, not a tidy-up.
drivers/net
net
